    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96001.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 38,
    "turn_text": "We had a lot of different issues in the city of Saratoga Springs where the housing market hit us pretty hard, a growing city, where we had a $3.5 million shortfall. Now, a lot of people would look at that and say, oh, that's nothing. But for a growing city that doesn't have a big budget, that's you're starting to count how much it costs for you to have a role of stamps. You're starting to count. Everything makes a big difference. And so we had to really look at what our priorities are. And what's great about municipal government is that you have to actually balance your budgets. You can't just deficit spend. You have to come together and figure out what your priorities are. And when you're looking at cutting things, You're not talking about really just cutting everything. You're talking about what is done at the appropriate levels. Where could we save money? Where can we get volunteers? What can we do as a community? And the entire community gets involved. For a library, for instance, our library was completely funded by volunteers when we first started. The community came in, businesses in the community came in, and we had a full-blown library that was started with people. And not only were we building a library, I have to tell you that that experience was incredible to me because we were building a community. You started seeing people come together for the same cause. They started going to, you know, cafe a lago on Mondays because they knew that the proceeds would go to",
