    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96001.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 102,
    "turn_text": "well. You know, my philosophy on education is this. I think we need to give our local teachers, our local school boards as much room to grow as possible. They have to be able to use their talents. I can tell you with working with our teachers, they sacrifice so much. They're underpaid and they're told how to educate. And when the school system fails, they blame it on the teachers. They're told what to do. I mean, it's really unfair. I think we need to allow our teachers to use their talents and their gifts because I can tell you right now, most of the teachers that are out there, they're not doing it for the money. They're doing it because they have a passion and they love that space and they want to be able to influence and help grow the future of this country. And I think that we need to give them as much ability to do that as possible. So instead of, you know, you've got groups in Washington that think that they can know it better, that they can do a better job. There isn't anyone that's more experienced than that teacher in that classroom working with the parents of that student. And we also have to know that our children all learn differently. It's not a one size fits all. Three of my kids. have a different way of being educated. My son needs a little bit more movement and stimulation when he is learning. He can't just sit down and listen. He's got to be able to move around a little bit. I'm sure people would say that he's got some issues with attention, but his teacher has worked really well with him where when he's answering a question, he can have a ball that he throws up or does you know, throws up in the air. My oldest daughter is pretty self-motivated. She can do everything on her own. She doesn't need us to do anything. My middle child, like I said, is goal-oriented. You give her something to work for, and she's there. And they have to be, and it's like, I guess I'm like that too, because to me, I'm a runner. I can see if I can get to certain miles, and I can get to that. to that, it allows me to move like, oh, I can do three, well, I can do another three, and I can do another three, and I just need to get to that point. But kids learn differently, and we have to be able to give our teachers the ability to teach in those classrooms. Well,",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
