    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96001.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 115,
    "turn_text": "I think that's what's really going to help fix some of these problems. Right now, a lot of the vehicles, we're making some movements in that area, but a lot of the vehicles that are a little cleaner are more expensive. And how can we ask a family that makes $20,000 a year to buy a Tesla or something? It's not going to work. It's not going to happen. So I think that there's a lot that we can do. I think that the framework, I think tax reform is a big part of that too in promoting and allowing for clean energy. I mean, just think about it. A lot of the companies that have been doing a lot of explorations on wind or solar or even cars, they're a lot cleaner. We have to be able to let people invest in that infrastructure. We have to be able to let them invest in that technology. So if you've got an S-Corp or if you've got a corporation and we're taxing them at 44% or we're taxing them at, if it's an S-Corp, 44% or if it's a corporation, 35%, how are they going to put more money into jobs? How are they going to put more money into innovation? I think this is a really good conversation for all of us to have. The Citizens' Climate Lobby, we had a town hall with that group, and they really opened my eyes and changed my heart really on how we approached this issue. And I think, again, we as individuals have a responsibility to make it better. And I think Utah could be a prime example of how we work on both sides of the aisle for a common cause. And that is to not just leave our country economically viable, but also environmentally viable.",
