    "turn_idx": 136,
    "turn_text": "services. And I am on financial institutions, subcommittee, terrorism and illicit financing and monetary policy and trade. And one of the things that I think hurts that effort is the lack of access to credit. So if you look at rural areas, they don't have the JP Morgan Chase. They have these small community banks that are seriously withering on a vine and they can't get the access to credit that they need. So most of the time, they're leaving. They're leaving their cities. They're leaving their rural areas. Washington sometimes has to take responsibility for the fact that they've created an environment where they're incentivizing the wrong behavior. You know, we have to, again, get those resources down to those areas. The other thing that we have to do is allow those cities. Like, I worked with John Curtis on this when we'd go to mag and we would map out transportation. I was a big supporter of making sure that we had areas where people could bike and people could run and we had trails that were connected. So that way people had an option. Right now, the option in Utah is very limited. And so people will do what they need to do to get to and from work. And so we have to open up access to credit and we open up access to different options so that people can actually have a choice.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
