    "turn_idx": 152,
    "turn_text": "have to have that ability. And the White House, their job is to execute what the legislative branch does. That's what it's supposed to be. And then the obviously the judicial branch is supposed to decide whether it's constitutional or not. People aren't supposed to drive out of their lanes, but it seems like everybody's driving everywhere. And that's where you get a lot of the frustration from. So let me talk about immigration for a second because it's one of the things that really frustrates me. Immigration should be something that brings us all together. It should be, because if you think about it, every single person in this country, someone in their ancestry immigrated. We're all part of an ancestry. We had somebody in the past that has immigrated to the United States. So I think what's really frustrating is this is the political side that is pinning one against the other, just so that somebody can claim a voter block, and that's not how things should be done. Now, I can tell you right now that I am a product of immigration. I was born in the United States. I was born in New York. My parents immigrated to the United States in the 70s. They were able to do it legally. And I have to say that this country has to take responsibility for the fact that the immigration problem that we have is a result of Washington. It's a result of Washington not promoting good immigration policies. My dad, when he immigrated to the United States, he would describe his naturalization ceremony to me. He said, I learned American history. I learned about the Constitution. As a matter of fact, I probably knew more about American history than most Americans did at the time. And when I pledged my allegiance to the American flag, I knew exactly what I was saying, and I meant every word of it. I think every American, first of all, before they have an opinion about immigration should take the same vows that people who have immigrated to the United States and gaining their citizenship take during that naturalization ceremony because it's an allegiance to the United States that I think should be taken by every American. How are you feeling about DACA? Okay. So DACA, to me, DACA was a bandaid. I think to say that, hey, by the way, we're going to say it's okay for somebody to be here illegally wasn't the right step. Nobody wants to do something or wants to be here illegally. I think people want to be able to have something that no one can take away because as we can see, what is given by the administration can also be taken away by the administration. Do",
